<!--yes, in Nahuatl, the final phoneme cluster is /tɬ/ rather than /tl/; however /l/ is the only lateral consonant in English and is as close as a monolingual English speaker is going to get-->
The '''dual macuahuitl''' (in English pronounced roughly {{Respell|mah|KWAH|weetl}}) is a [[non-degradable]] two-handed [[spiked]] weapon that requires level 70 [[Attack]] and 75 [[Strength]] to equip, which is obtained as a possible reward from the [[Lunar Chest]] in [[Neypotzli]].

The weapon [[Multi-hit weapons|hits twice per attack]], spaced 1 game tick apart when attacking monsters, with its [[Maximum melee hit|maximum hit]] split out over both [[hitsplats]]. The first hit takes half, rounded down, and the second hit takes the remainder, meaning a maximum hit of 41 will be split into two maximum hits of 20 and 21.{{CiteDiscord|author=Mod Ash|url=https://discord.com/channels/324132423636090880/434286997360738305/1551904659164110869|date=2026-09-22|quote=As always, these answers are "_provided I'm reading my colleagues' code correctly_"!

> Your max hit is split across both splats. The first splat takes 50% (rounded down), and the second splat the rest the remainder
Yes.

> If you fail accuracy on the first splat, the second splat is guaranteed to fail accuracy
Yes.

> Passing accuracy on a splat has a 1/3 chance to activate the set effect.
Yes.

> Where passing accuracy on both splats has a 1 - 2/3 x 2/3 = 5/9 chance to activate the set effect
Not quite. If you passed on both splats:
It rolls a value 0-99 inclusive.
It rolls a second value 0-99 inclusive, and takes whichever of these two values was lower.
If the resulting value is 32 or below, the effect triggers.|archiveurl=|archivedate=|ctxquote=|notes=|name=Ash Regular attack}}

Each hitsplat has its own accuracy check. However, unlike other two-hit weapons like [[Torag's hammers]] or [[sulphur blades]], where both checks are independent (either can hit or miss without affecting the other, equivalent to two separate hits with a single-hit weapon), for the Dual macuahuitl the second accuracy check is guaranteed to fail if the first one failed. As a result, the second hitsplat effectively requires two consecutive successful accuracy checks, lowering its expected damage output compared to the first.<ref name="Ash Regular attack"/>

This is an intentional design{{CiteVideo|name=maracas|site=youtube|title=Fortis Colosseum & Perilous Moons Q&A Livestream!|timestamp=2253s|author=Old School RuneScape|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n9py_1vGiMQ&t=2253s|date=27 October 2023}} to push the weapon into a niche against targets with lower [[Defence]] levels and mirrors the attacks of the [[Moons of Peril]], which check accuracy in the same way.

As a slight counterbalance to this drawback, the dual macuahuitl has relatively high attack bonuses for its attack speed and level requirements. Its [[Crush]] bonus substantially exceeds that of the [[abyssal bludgeon]] and rivals the combined crush bonus of an [[inquisitor's mace]] and [[avernic defender]].

